---
// 测评文章 #5：视频门铃
// 数据来源（2026-11-19 核实）：Google Store 官方规格页与定价、TP-Link 美国官网 D130/D225 规格页、Wirecutter 2026-08-05 智能门铃指南、Tom's Guide 视频门铃榜、Trusted Reviews / MakeUseOf 单机评测、Amazon 买家评论
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Best Video Doorbells for Wired Porches and Rentals — DealWillow"
  description="The best video doorbells in the US right now: the Google Nest Doorbell (Wired, 3rd Gen) for most porches, the TP-Link Tapo D130 if you refuse a monthly bill, and the Tapo D225 if there is no wiring at all."
>
  <main class="wrap article">
    <a class="crumb" href="/">&larr; All reviews</a>

    <div class="head">
      <span class="cat-tag">Cameras</span>
      <h1>Best Video Doorbells for Wired Porches and Rentals</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated Nov 2026 · Prices checked Nov 19, 2026 · US models only</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lede">
      <p>
        <strong>If your door already has doorbell wiring, buy the Google Nest Doorbell (Wired, 3rd Gen).</strong>
        It is the top pick in Wirecutter's August 2026 guide and Tom's Guide's best-overall slot, and the
        spec sheet explains why: a 2K square sensor, a 166-degree diagonal view that catches both a face
        and a box on the mat, and on-device alerts that separate people from cars without a subscription.
        If a monthly fee is the thing you refuse to pay, the <strong>TP-Link Tapo D130</strong> records
        continuously to a memory card for about $60. If there is no wiring and no electrician in your
        future, that is the <strong>Tapo D225</strong> — a battery doorbell that still stores clips for free.
      </p>
    </div>


    <h2 class="block-title">Everything we recommend</h2>
    <div class="picks">
      <a class="pick" href="#nest">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best overall</span>
        <span class="pick-role">2K square view, best motion sorting</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Google Nest Doorbell (Wired, 3rd Gen)</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$199.99 · Google Store</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#d130">
        <span class="pick-tag alt">Best without a subscription</span>
        <span class="pick-role">microSD recording, chime in the box</span>
        <span class="pick-name">TP-Link Tapo D130</span>
        <span class="pick-price">~$55–60 · TP-Link / Amazon</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#d225">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best battery pick</span>
        <span class="pick-role">No wiring needed, free local storage</span>
        <span class="pick-name">TP-Link Tapo D225</span>
        <span class="pick-price">~$85–100 · Amazon</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ 1. Nest ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="nest">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best overall</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">1. Google Nest Doorbell (Wired, 3rd Gen)</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Video</span><b>2K (2048 × 2048), 1:1, 30 fps, HDR</b></div>
        <div><span>Field of view</span><b>166° diagonal · 6x digital zoom</b></div>
        <div><span>Power</span><b>Hardwired, 16–24 VAC · 10–40 VA</b></div>
        <div><span>Free recording</span><b>6 hours of event previews, 10-second clips</b></div>
        <div><span>With a plan</span><b>30 days ($10/mo) or 60 days ($20/mo)</b></div>
        <div><span>Night vision</span><b>IR only, ~10 ft — no color mode</b></div>
        <div><span>Weather / size</span><b>IP65 · 5.2 × 1.7 × 1.1 in · 4.9 oz</b></div>
        <div><span>Warranty</span><b>1 year</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Top pick in both Wirecutter's August 2026 guide and Tom's Guide's rankings</li>
            <li>Square 1:1 frame is the right shape for a doorstep — head and parcel in one shot</li>
            <li>Person, animal, vehicle and package alerts run on the device, no plan required</li>
            <li>Clips can run up to five minutes, longer than the other picks here</li>
            <li>Rings most existing wired chimes; the chime connector is in the box</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Free tier is nearly useless for evidence: 10-second clips, deleted after 6 hours</li>
            <li>$10/month is the most expensive single-camera plan among these picks</li>
            <li>Needs a 16–24 VAC transformer — old or weak doorbell wiring may need replacing</li>
            <li>The 1:1 crop cannot be widened, and there is no color night vision</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          The reason this doorbell keeps winning 2026 roundups is not the resolution — plenty of rivals
          claim 2K — it's the shape of the frame. A 2048 × 2048 square with a 166-degree diagonal view
          puts a visitor's face and the parcel at their feet in the same shot, which is the entire job.
          Wirecutter's testers rate it the most accurate of the doorbells they ran at telling people,
          animals, vehicles and packages apart, and the alert filtering is done on the device rather than
          in a cloud service.
        </p>
        <p>
          The honest catch is the subscription. Google's free tier keeps six hours of ten-second event
          previews, which is enough to know someone came by and not enough to identify them a day later.
          Google Home Premium Standard ($10/month or $100/year) extends event history to 30 days and
          Advanced ($20/month) adds 60 days plus up to 10 days of 24/7 continuous recording. Over three
          years the plan costs more than the doorbell, and that math is the single most common complaint
          in owner reviews — usually phrased as four stars instead of five.
        </p>
        <p>
          Installation is where buyers get surprised. This is a hardwired unit that wants a 16–24 VAC,
          10–40 VA transformer; if yours is old, undersized or missing, Google sells an indoor power
          adapter, but then your wired chime stops working. Check the transformer before you order, not
          after. The other adjustment is the app: this doorbell requires the Google Home app and will not
          work with the legacy Nest app, and owners describe the event-history menus as clicky.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the one to buy if you have wiring, want the best alerts, and can live with a monthly bill.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 2. Tapo D130 ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="d130">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best without a subscription</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">2. TP-Link Tapo D130</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Video</span><b>2K 5MP (2560 × 1920), 4:3</b></div>
        <div><span>Field of view</span><b>180° diagonal · 172° H · 144° V</b></div>
        <div><span>Power</span><b>Hardwired only, 8–24 VAC · 10 VA min</b></div>
        <div><span>Storage</span><b>microSD to 512 GB · free 24/7 recording</b></div>
        <div><span>Detection</span><b>Person, pet, vehicle, package — no fee</b></div>
        <div><span>Chime</span><b>Plug-in chime included</b></div>
        <div><span>Wi-Fi / weather</span><b>2.4 GHz only · IP65</b></div>
        <div><span>Size</span><b>3.35 × 1.67 × 1.28 in</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Continuous recording to a memory card with no plan at all — Wirecutter's budget pick</li>
            <li>Tall 4:3 frame at 180 degrees; head-to-toe without a fisheye pancake</li>
            <li>Detection for people, pets, vehicles and packages stays free</li>
            <li>Chime included, which Ring and Blink usually charge extra for</li>
            <li>Roughly a third the price of the Nest, with better night color</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Night footage is the weak spot — owners say it needs the spotlight on</li>
            <li>2.4 GHz Wi-Fi only, which is the crowded band on most streets</li>
            <li>Notification thumbnails and richer alerts are locked behind Tapo Care</li>
            <li>Hardwired only; no battery option if the wiring is missing</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          For about $60 you get the two features that actually matter at a front door: a frame tall
          enough to show a face and a parcel at once, and footage that keeps recording whether or not you
          pay anyone monthly. A 2K 5MP sensor in a 4:3 crop with a 180-degree diagonal view is a smarter
          trade than a wider 16:9 frame, because the pixels go where the doorstep is instead of the
          brickwork either side. Slot in a card and the D130 records around the clock — TP-Link pegs
          512 GB at roughly 390 hours of 2K footage.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cross-check the listings and you find a small price spread rather than a disagreement:
          TP-Link's own US store lists $59.99 against a $64.99 list price and was running $55.99 at the
          time of writing, while Wirecutter quoted $54 at Amazon in August 2026. Any of those is the
          same purchase. What you cannot do at this price is expect polish: rich notifications with a
          thumbnail image require Tapo Care ($3.50/month per camera), and the free tier gives you a
          motion alert without the preview.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two caveats worth taking seriously. First, night image quality is the most repeated complaint
          across owner reviews and third-party write-ups — with the spotlight off, several describe the
          picture as soft or pixelated, so a porch light matters here more than the spec sheet suggests.
          Second, sources disagree about the chime: TP-Link's box contents list jumpers for an existing
          chime, while one hardwired-doorbell roundup states the old mechanical chime is not reused and
          you get the bundled plug-in chime instead. We could not settle that from published material —
          if keeping your existing bell matters, confirm with TP-Link support before ordering.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the value pick, and the only one here that costs nothing to run after you buy a memory card.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 3. Tapo D225 ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="d225">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best battery pick</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">3. TP-Link Tapo D225</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Video</span><b>2K QHD · 180° diagonal, 4:3</b></div>
        <div><span>Power</span><b>10,000 mAh battery or hardwired</b></div>
        <div><span>Battery life</span><b>8 months claimed · ~4 months per Wirecutter</b></div>
        <div><span>Recharge</span><b>~5 hours, doorbell off the wall</b></div>
        <div><span>Storage</span><b>microSD to 512 GB · Tapo Care $3.50/mo</b></div>
        <div><span>Wired mode</span><b>24/7 recording to card</b></div>
        <div><span>Weather / size</span><b>IP66 · 5.91 × 1.97 × 1.52 in</b></div>
        <div><span>Notable gap</span><b>No HDR</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Wirecutter's top battery-powered pick for 2026</li>
            <li>Installs without wiring, then converts to 24/7 recording if you hardwire it later</li>
            <li>Free person, vehicle, package and pet detection plus local storage</li>
            <li>Ringing it places a phone call, so answering does not mean opening an app</li>
            <li>Chime included, and the cloud plan is cheap if you want one</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>No HDR, so a sunlit walkway blows out while the doorway goes dark</li>
            <li>Noticeable fisheye distortion at the edges of the 180-degree view</li>
            <li>The battery is not removable — the whole unit comes off to charge</li>
            <li>Bulky next to a Nest, and busy streets drain the battery far faster than 8 months</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          If there is no doorbell wiring, this is the least compromised way out. It carries a
          10,000 mAh battery, keeps the same tall 180-degree head-to-toe framing as the D130, and does
          its detection on board — so the free experience is a doorbell that works, not a demo. Ring it
          and the app places a call to your phone rather than asking you to open anything, which sounds
          trivial until you are carrying groceries.
        </p>
        <p>
          Battery claims need translating. TP-Link rates eight months; Wirecutter's tester, with what it
          calls a typical amount of motion, charged every four months. Trusted Reviews saw the charge
          fall 15% in five days on a busy city porch, which points to roughly a month in that setting.
          Recharging means pulling the whole doorbell off its mount for about five hours, because the
          cell is sealed in. Hardwire it and the math changes completely: you get 24/7 recording to the
          card, and the battery becomes backup rather than a chore.
        </p>
        <p>
          Image quality is where reviewers split, and the split is instructive. Wirecutter calls the 2K
          picture sharp and the field of view expansive; MakeUseOf scored it 6/10 on video and named the
          missing HDR as the dealbreaker, with a sunlit path washing out while the covered porch stayed
          dark. Both can be true of the same doorbell on different porches. If your entryway is deeply
          shaded or faces bright afternoon sun, expect the weaker result, and expect some edge
          distortion from the wide lens either way. Package detection was inconsistent in that same
          review — a dozen deliveries, no package alerts — so treat it as a bonus, not a guarantee.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the right doorbell when drilling and wiring are off the table, as long as your porch is not a lighting torture test.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Also worth knowing</h2>
      <p>
        If you already run wired security cameras, the <strong>Reolink Video Doorbell PoE</strong> is
        the one that fits an existing system rather than competing with it: one Ethernet cable carries
        power and data, clips land on a microSD card or a Reolink NVR with no monthly fee, and it slots
        into the same recorder as the brand's outdoor cameras — see our
        <a href="/review/best-home-security-cameras/">home security camera guide</a> for the REOLINK
        models we cover there. Two things to know before ordering: Reolink's PoE and Wi-Fi doorbells will
        not ring an old mechanical chime, and the price is genuinely unsettled — a November 6, 2026
        check of Reolink's US store put the PoE model at $119.99, a Reolink blog post cites $75.99, and
        regional storefronts list $94.99. Verify at checkout.
      </p>
      <p>
        Also circulating in 2026 roundups: the <strong>eufy E340</strong> (about $180, dual camera with a
        downward lens for packages, local storage with no required plan) and the <strong>Ring Wired
        Doorbell Pro, 3rd Gen</strong> (4K, about $180, the sharpest video in the category but the
        deepest subscription lock-in). Neither was cross-checked against primary lab data for this
        guide, so treat both as leads rather than picks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">How we picked</h2>
      <p>
        We started from Wirecutter's August 5, 2026 doorbell guide and Tom's Guide's 2026 rankings,
        then verified resolution, field of view, power requirements, storage and subscription terms on
        Google's and TP-Link's own US product pages. Prices were checked on <strong>Nov 19, 2026</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Before you buy: two things almost nobody tells you</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Check the transformer, not just the wiring.</strong> A doorbell that needs 16–24 VAC and
        10–40 VA will misbehave — reboots, drop Wi-Fi, refuse to chime — on a transformer that is old or
        undersized, and that is a $20 part plus an afternoon you did not plan for. The Tapo D130 is the
        more forgiving of the two wired picks here, asking only for 8–24 VAC at 10 VA.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Price the subscription over five years, not one.</strong> The Nest's free tier keeps
        ten-second clips for six hours; useful cloud history costs $10 a month, or $600 over five years
        on top of the hardware. A $60 doorbell with a $20 memory card and no plan is the cheaper
        five-year decision, and a worse one the week you actually need to identify someone. Decide which
        of those two you are buying before you compare sticker prices.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

